# Ethical Considerations

As developers of Folio, we recognise that our design decisions affect how people discover local music, which artists receive attention and how users participate in discussions. Our aim is to create a more intentional and community-focused discovery experience. However, human curation and social interaction introduce ethical responsibilities that we must address throughout development.

## Curatorial Bias and Fair Representation

Folio replaces algorithmic recommendations with human-curated daily Editions. While this makes the person behind each selection more visible, it does not remove bias. Curators may favour familiar genres, established artists, particular venues or people within their own networks. With only 8–12 picks per Edition, these decisions influence whose work becomes visible.

We therefore recognise the need for transparent curator approval criteria, varied perspectives and disclosure of personal or financial connections to featured artists. Each Edition should be understood as a curator’s selection rather than a complete representation of the city’s music scene. Reviewing selections over time would help us identify communities or genres that are repeatedly overlooked.

## Accuracy and Transparency

Users should be able to trust the information presented in Folio. We require sources for Liner Notes and enforce this requirement through the database. However, including a source does not automatically make a statement accurate. Sources can be incomplete, outdated or based on the same unsupported information.

Some seed picks currently rely on Wikipedia alone and require further cross-checking. We should clearly distinguish factual information from a curator’s personal interpretation and provide a way for artists and users to request corrections. Although Editions lock at midnight, this should not prevent us from correcting inaccurate or harmful content through a visible amendment process.

## Privacy and User Control

We allow users to read without an account and join as guests without providing an email address. This reduces the information required to participate, but guest access should not be presented as a guarantee of complete anonymity.

Our Nearby feature uses optional location access. Users should understand why this permission is requested and retain a useful browsing experience if they decline. Before wider release, we would need clear information about what data is processed, how long it is retained and how users can request deletion of their accounts or contributions. We should collect only information necessary for the app’s purpose.

## Community Safety and Moderation

Comments can help listeners, artists and curators connect, but they can also enable harassment, discrimination, spam and personal attacks. Restricting discussion to the current Edition limits when comments can be posted, but it does not prevent abuse.

Moderation has been considered in our design, but the necessary tooling is not yet implemented. Before broader public use, we would need clear community guidelines, accessible reporting tools and a fair process for reviewing harmful content and appeals. Guest participation should remain approachable while including safeguards against repeated abuse. Artist–fan connections should also respect artists’ privacy and personal boundaries.

## Artist Rights and Creative Ownership

We removed streaming from our scope because of licensing concerns. However, this does not resolve every issue involving creative ownership. Photographs, artwork, lyrics and other materials may still require permission before being reproduced.

We should credit creators accurately, check permissions and provide a process for correction or removal requests. Featuring an artist should not imply that they endorse Folio. Our responsibility is to support discovery while respecting artists’ control over their work and public representation.

## AI-Made Music and Authenticity

Folio cannot reliably detect AI-made music. Our current safeguard requires a named curator to sign each new-artist pick and provide two independent sources. This creates accountability, but it cannot prevent every mistake or dishonest declaration.

We should therefore avoid presenting these checks as proof of authenticity. Any concerns should be investigated fairly without making unsupported accusations about artists. We would also need a clear explanation of how Folio approaches AI-assisted creation, recognising that different uses of AI raise different ethical questions. Deliberate abuse of our safeguards has not yet been tested.

## Cultural Respect and Inclusion

Our selections help shape how newcomers understand the local music scene. We should avoid presenting one group’s tastes or experiences as representative of everyone in the city. This includes considering migrant communities, international artists and less visible music scenes.

When presenting Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander music, histories or cultural knowledge, we should seek appropriate guidance and consider permissions, attribution and context. Public availability does not necessarily mean that cultural information is appropriate for us to reproduce or interpret. Our Acknowledgement of Country has not yet received appropriate review, and we recognise this as unfinished work.

## Accessibility and Participation

An app cannot support community participation if people struggle to use it. During evaluation, both participants were unable to find how to comment. We responded with a Discussion shortcut, clearer guest participation prompts and larger tap targets.

These changes address a specific finding, but they do not establish that Folio is accessible to everyone. Further testing should consider screen-reader support, keyboard navigation, colour contrast, text resizing and clear language. Because Folio is intended to help newcomers, we should also avoid assuming prior knowledge of Brisbane’s venues or music culture.

## Attention and User Autonomy

We removed likes and rankings and limited Folio to one daily Edition to encourage intentional discovery. However, other features may still influence users’ behaviour. The midnight lock could create pressure to participate before the day ends, while the “reading now” count could encourage users to associate activity with value.

The count can currently be inflated through repeated calls, so it should not be described as an accurate measure of unique readers. We need further evaluation to understand whether it supports shared presence or creates unnecessary social pressure. Sharing and participation should remain voluntary and user-controlled.

## Research Ethics and Developer Accountability

We have a responsibility to report our research honestly. Our evaluation involved two short sessions with people known to the team, and some questions were leading. One earlier interview was a team member’s self-interview, which we treat as weaker evidence. These findings are useful for improving the prototype but do not prove that Folio meets the needs of a wider community.

Near-live comments, sharing and the reading count were added after participant evaluation. Although technically tested, their social effects remain untested with participants. Future research should involve informed, voluntary participation, protect participants’ identities and include a broader range of users.

As developers, we remain responsible for reviewing our decisions and responding to concerns. Moderation, privacy controls, content permissions, cultural review and broader accessibility testing are necessary areas of further work before public release.
